questions.text:
(a) whether Government proposes to undertake structural reforms to strengthen coordination among CSIR laboratories and industry for accelerating commercialization of indigenous technologies;
(b) whether Government has studied global best practices, particularly South Korea's statutory framework governing national research institutions;
(c) whether Government proposes to enact a comprehensive legislation for CSIR covering governance, technology transfer, intellectual property management, industry partnerships, performance accountability and commercialization of publicly funded research; and
(d) whether Government has created a whole-of-Government mechanism to ensure mandatory evaluation, adoption and scaling up of CSIR-developed technologies by Central Ministries, State Governments, Public Sector Undertakings and If so, the details thereof?

(a) whether the National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA) has assessed the emerging threat of biological attacks and large-scale bio-emergencies in the country;
(b) whether a comprehensive national preparedness and response framework has been developed in coordination with State Governments and concerned Ministries for prevention, early detection, containment and coordinated response to such threats;
(c) what specific details govern this strategy, particularly regarding hospital surge capacity, mass medical treatment counter-measures, emergency vaccine/medicine stockpiles and the deployment of specialized National Disaster Response Force (NDRF) units; and
(d) the details of regular mock drills conducted and protocols established to ensure community awareness and rapid civil defense response?

(a) whether Government has assessed the therapeutic potential and public-health relevance of regenerative medicine, including stem cell therapy, gene therapy, tissue engineering and biomaterials, for diseases with limited treatment options;
(b) whether international best practices, including regulatory frameworks adopted by countries such as South Korea, have been examined;
(c) whether Government proposes to establish a dedicated, ethical and time-bound regulatory framework to promote research, clinical trials and indigenous manufacturing in this sector; and
(d) whether measures are under consideration to reduce high development costs and ensure affordable patient access to regenerative therapies?

(a) whether Government has undertaken a comparative review of global best practices, particularly those adopted in the UAE and Estonia, to further strengthen India's statutory audit and public-interest oversight framework;
(b) whether Government proposes to introduce reforms such as a national digital audit registry, mandatory cooling-off periods for statutory auditors, AI-enabled risk-based inspections and fixed audit quality review mechanisms; and
(c) whether Government is considering measures to strengthen technology-driven audit supervision, cyber and forensic audit standards, auditor independence and transparency to enhance investor confidence, financial stability and public trust in corporate financial reporting?

(a) whether the Central Government, in coordination with the State Governments, has established any mechanism to inspect the structural integrity and safety of high-tension (HT) electricity transmission lines and poles across the country, particularly in view of accidents caused by their collapse;
(b) if so, the details thereof;
(c) whether Government has made it mandatory for electricity utilities/distribution companies to carry out periodic inspections of high-tension electricity transmission lines and poles and submit reports on their condition; and
(d) if so, the periodicity of such inspections and the mechanism in place to monitor compliance with these requirements?

(a) whether Government proposes to modernise fire safety infrastructure at railway stations in Maharashtra to effectively respond to fire incidents involving passenger trains, goods trains and station premises;
(b) the details of fire detection, suppression, evacuation systems, firefighting equipment and trained emergency response personnel available at major railway stations in Maharashtra, including Hazur Sahib Nanded Railway Station; and
(c) whether, in view of the year-round heavy pilgrim traffic at Takht Sri Hazur Sahib, Government proposes to strengthen and upgrade the fire safety and emergency response system at Hazur Sahib Nanded Railway Station?

(a) whether Government has received any proposal or representation for the introduction of a Vande Bharat Sleeper train between Hazur Sahib Nanded and Amritsar to provide faster and more comfortable rail connectivity for thousands of Sikh devotees travelling between two of Sikh pilgrimage centres;
(b) whether Government proposes to introduce new Vande Bharat train services from Hazur Sahib Nanded to Hyderabad and from Hazur Sahib Nanded to Pune, considering the substantial passenger demand, business and tourism traffic on these routes; and
(c) if so, the details thereof, including the present status, feasibility studies undertaken, expected timelines for commencement of these services?

(a) whether Government has taken note of the judgment dated 09 July, 2026 of the Hon'ble Madras High Court (Madurai Bench) regarding higher pension under EPS, 1995 for employees of exempted establishments;
(b) whether Government has examined the implementation of the judgments of the Hon'ble Supreme Court, including Sunil Kumar B. & Others, which apply to all eligible employees under EPS, 1995, irrespective of whether they belong to exempted or un-exempted establishments; and
(c) the steps taken or proposed to ensure time-bound disposal of eligible higher pension claims, reduce avoidable litigation and equitable treatment to all eligible pensioners, including employees of exempted establishments?

(a) whether Government is aware of persistent consumer grievances regarding low speeds, latency and network congestion, particularly in rural and semi-urban areas;
(b) whether there is a proposal to transition from TRAI guidelines to a legally enforceable statutory framework ensuring minimum internet speed, mandatory real-time performance disclosures, and automatic consumer compensation for service deficiencies;
(c) the technical steps being taken to regulate network performance parameters, specifically packet drop rates, automated bandwidth throttling by internet service providers (ISPs); and
(d) whether Government plans to deploy decentralized, independent automated probes to cross-verify ISP-claimed uptimes and introduce statutory financial penalties for disparities in service quality?

(a) whether Government has examined the need for a comprehensive National Livestock Development Act, considering that the existing framework mainly covers food safety and scheme-based interventions without a unified mechanism for livestock development, biosecurity, breeding, traceability, animal welfare and investment promotion;
(b) whether any study or Committee has examined international best practices, including the livestock legislation of the Republic of Korea, if so, the details thereof;
(c) whether Government proposes to formulate a National Livestock Development Policy (NLDP) or enact such legislation in consultation with States and stakeholders; and
(d) if so, the timeline thereof, if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government has assessed the absence of a uniform and enforceable national framework regulating maximum daily and weekly working hours of doctors in Government and private hospitals and its impact on patient safety and quality care;
(b) whether international best practices adopted in the USA, UK, Japan and Hong Kong and the working-hour circulars issued by the State Government of Kerala, have been examined in this context; and
(c) whether Government proposes to formulate national guidelines, in consultation with State Governments and medical bodies, to regulate doctors’ working hours and strengthen ethical medical practices, including control of unethical medical advertising?

(a) whether Government has initiated or proposes to facilitate collaboration between the Central Power Research Institute and the Government of Maharashtra for testing,research, and performance evaluation of electrical equipment used by State power utilities;
(b) whether, in view of the significant investments being made by the Government of Maharashtra in modernizing ageing transmission and distribution infrastructure, the Government has advised the State to utilize the testing, quality certification, and technical expertise of CPRI; and
(c) whether Government proposes to formulate a national framework encouraging all State Governments to collaborate with CPRI for electrical equipment testing, power sector research?

(a) whether Government proposes to formulate a comprehensive National Farm Mechanization Policy, drawing upon global best practices from South Korea, Japan, the United States, Israel and Australia, including establishment of a national institutional mechanism for research;
(b) whether Government intends to promote intelligent agricultural robots, women-friendly farm implements, skill development and encourage domestic and foreign private investment to accelerate mechanization, particularly for marginal farmers; and
(c) whether Government proposes to establish collaborative research, technology transfer and commercialization partnerships with South Korea and other technologically advanced countries to strengthen India's farm mechanization ecosystem, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government has received recommendations, including from the Kerala State Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities, to recognise Indian Sign Language (ISL) as the 23rd official language under the Eighth Schedule to the Constitution;
(b) whether the proposal is under examination in the Department of Official Language, Ministry of Home Affairs and if so, the details thereof; and
(c) whether Government proposes to hold structured consultations with State Governments, experts and representatives of the deaf community to examine the feasibility and modalities of according such recognition and if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government proposes to enact a comprehensive National Livestock Development legislation or policy framework, drawing upon global best practices, including those of South Korea and Vanuatu, to transform India's largely unorganized livestock sector into a scientifically regulated, competitive industry;
(b) whether the proposed framework will be formulated in consultation with State Governments, veterinary councils and other stakeholders to promote scientific livestock development and attract domestic and foreign investment; and
(c) the steps being taken to leverage the livestock sector, which provides employment to about 20.5 million people, for enhancing farmers' incomes, exports, ensuring nutritional security and achieving rural economic growth?

(a) whether Government has examined regulatory reforms in the United Kingdom and Australia, governing cosmetic surgery and whether it proposes to establish a dedicated framework based on risk classification of procedures to strengthen patient safety;
(b) whether the proposed framework would provide for mandatory licensing of all establishments, uniform qualification and credentialing standards for practitioners, protection of the title "cosmetic surgeon"; and
(c) whether Government proposes to regulate misleading advertisements and social media promotions, prescribe minimum age and enhance informed consent requirements, establish a national registry of cosmetic procedures and introduce a robust inspection and penalty to curb unauthorized practice?

(a) whether Government is aware of the absence of a comprehensive national regulatory framework governing dental implants, implant bio materials, artificial teeth and implantology practices, resulting in potential patient-safety risks;
(b) whether Government proposes to mandate scientific evaluation of implant devices, including fatigue, bio-compatibility, sterility and corrosion testing, and establish a National Dental Implant Registry with mandatory device traceability and adverse-event reporting, on the lines of international best practices; and
(c) whether Government proposes to prescribe mandatory practitioner certification, accreditation of implant centers, standardized informed-consent protocols and ethical advertising norms for dental implant procedures, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government is aware of the recurring fire tragedies across the country, including the tragic fire incident in Delhi's Vivek Vihar resulting in the loss of innocent lives;
(b) whether Government has assessed the contribution of overloading and ageing wiring, to fire accidents;
(c) whether Government reviewed the effectiveness of the existing mechanism for inspection of consumer electrical installations under the Central Electricity Authority Regulations;
(d) whether Government proposes to introduce mandatory periodic third-party electrical safety inspection; and
(e) whether Government proposes to establish a digital reporting and compliance mechanism for electrical safety inspections, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether India’s forest and tree cover together accounts for 25.17% of the total geographical area and that the forest protection is presently governed by Central and State laws, resulting in fragmented management of ecologically interconnected forests;
(b) whether Government has examined the international best practices, including forest conservation legislation of the Republic of Korea, covering forest zoning, disease and pest monitoring, fire prevention and landslide mitigation; and
(c) whether Government proposes to constitute expert committee comprising forestry scientists, ecologists and State representatives to recommend amendments to existing forest laws for a uniform and scientific conservation framework?

(a) whether Government is aware of recurring fatal chemical factory accidents attributed to aging equipments, imported used industrial plants and inadequate technical investigation of accidents;
(b) whether Government proposes to establish a multidisciplinary forensic engineering mechanism to support investigation of factory accidents, strengthen prosecution and develop uniform national investigation protocols under the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020; and
(c) the steps taken to equip inspectorates with modern diagnostic tools, prescribe criminal liability for third-party auditors issuing false or negligent safety certifications, and mandate periodic residual-life assessment and timely replacement of critical industrial equipments?

(a) whether Government is considering to implement a real-time structural health monitoring and early warning systems to strengthen the safety of road tunnels;
(b) whether Government is considering to formulate standards for lighting and visibility-related safety in road and rail tunnels;
(c) the steps taken so far to implement uniform national tunnel safety standards for tunnels belonging to Indian Railways and State Governments; and
(d) whether specific standards have been prescribed regarding real-time monitoring, water ingress control, lighting and fire safety for tunnels under construction beneath rivers or the sea?

(a) whether Government is aware that there is no uniform national policy prescribing the maximum permissible number of surgical procedures per day in an Operation Theater (OT), and whether this has raised concerns regarding infection control;
(b) whether Government has examined reports of post-operative infections and patient deaths allegedly associated with excessive surgical workload, and whether existing practices conform to the safe surgery guidelines of the WHO;
(c) whether Government proposes to prescribe mandatory minimum intervals between consecutive surgeries; and
(d) whether Government proposes to mandate declaration of OT capacity based on infrastructure and type of surgeries, along with periodic compliance audits in hospitals?

(a) whether Government is aware of recent fire incidents caused by unauthorized excavation damaging underground gas and oil pipelines, particularly in remote areas;
(b) whether Government proposes to mandate all gas and oil pipeline operators to submit updated digital pipeline alignment maps, including AutoCAD/GIS formats, to local authorities, and integrate them with a centralized underground utility mapping system; and
(c) whether Government proposes to make prior permission mandatory for excavations exceeding 8 feet, after verification of underground utility maps, and to establish a "Call Before You Dig (CBuD)/One-Call" clearance mechanism to prevent accidental pipeline damage and safeguard critical energy infrastructure?

(a) whether Government is considering enactment of legislation mandating periodic structural safety audits of high-rise buildings, on the lines of statutory frameworks adopted in countries such as the United Kingdom and the Republic of Korea;
(b) whether any national mechanism exists to monitor the cumulative long-term impact of seismic activity and ageing on the structural foundation strength of buildings;
(c) the measures taken or proposed to mandate periodic post-construction structural inspections for early detection of structural deterioration and prevention of building collapses; and
(d) the timeline for framing and enforcing such legislation to detect early structural weakening, enhance public safety and prevent building collapses?
bs

(a) whether Government is aware that several traditional Indian musical instruments are on the verge of extinction, and there is an acute shortage of highly specialized artisans possessing rare skills of crafting and repairing such instruments;
(b) whether the shortage of such artisans poses loss to India’s musical heritage and indigenous systems;
(c) whether Government proposes to advise State Governments to identify and map such artisans and create database; and
(d) whether steps are being considered to promote skill transfer to younger generations and to provide suitable financial and social assistance to ensure their sustainability and dignity?

(a) whether Government proposes to undertake a division-wise resurvey and re-verification of railway land at each station and along railway tracks, in coordination with State Governments, to reconfirm revenue titles, boundaries, and land records of Indian Railways;
(b) the concrete steps taken division-wise, in coordination with State Governments and district administrations, to identify, prevent, and remove illegal encroachments from railway land; and
(c) whether Government has considered establishing dedicated, division-level railway land management and monitoring wings to regularly oversee land records, encroachments, disputes, and protection, if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government intends to establish a camp office of the Central Research Institute for Dryland Agriculture (CRIDA) at Nanded to provide hands-on training and field level guidance to farmers of the region, as many farmers are unable to travel to its Hyderabad office;
(b) if not, the reasons therefor;
(c) the number of new farming technologies and practices that have been developed by the Institute during the last three years; and
(d) whether the impact and field-level outcomes of these technologies have been independently evaluated through any neutral or accredited agency, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government has examined the growing mismatch between the large annual output of engineering graduates and limited demand in core economic sectors, resulting in low employability and under-utilisation of skilled youth;
(b) whether studies and data from AICTE and industry bodies on poor quality, outdated curricula, faculty shortages and closure of sub-standard engineering institutions have been assessed;
(c) whether Government proposes time-bound reforms under NEP, 2020 to rationalise intake, upgrade curriculum and faculty quality and strengthen industry-academia linkages; and
(d) whether targeted measures are being implemented to improve employability, reduce student distress and align engineering education with national development priorities?

(a) whether Government is aware that dental caries, periodontal disease, oral cancer, and edentulism constitute a major and growing public health burden, especially in rural and underserved regions;
(b) whether Government proposes to improve access to affordable dental care by addressing shortages of dental professionals and high treatment costs;
(c) whether nationwide epidemiological surveys, behavioural and dietary interventions, and targeted oral cancer prevention and early-detection programmes are being strengthened; and
(d) whether research and validation of indigenous medicinal plants for oral health promotion are being encouraged to support preventive and cost-effective care?
J

(a) the extant mechanism in place, including scheduled routine and surprise inspections, to ensure the availability of adequate firefighting systems at petrol pumps across the country;
(b) the number of petrol pumps penalised for inadequate firefighting arrangements during the last three years;
(c) the number of minor and major fire incidents reported at petrol pumps across the country during the last five years, and the action taken thereon; and
(d) the steps taken by Government, in coordination with Government of Maharashtra, to strengthen fire safety norms and emergency preparedness at petrol pumps, particularly in high-density urban areas such as Mumbai to ensuring safety?

(a) whether the Central Government is aware that despite the substantial production of cotton, soybean, pigeon pea and oilseeds in Marathwada, especially Nanded, value addition is being concentrated in other States due to the lack of processing industries and MSME clusters;
(b) whether Government is considering to constitute an Inter-Ministerial Task Force, comprising the Ministries of Agriculture and farmers welfare, food processing Industries and textiles, under the Ministry of MSME to develop this region as an agro-based industrial hub; and
(c) if so, its framework and timeline thereof, if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether the Department of Land Resources has taken cognizance of any representation seeking the establishment of a Unified Centralized Monitoring System to identify, monitor and remove encroachments on Central Government land across all States, irrespective of the administrative control of such land, if so, the details thereof;
(b) whether it is a fact that encroachments on Central Government properties exist in various States; and
(c) if so, the State-wise details thereof and the steps taken by Government, in coordination with the concerned State Governments, to remove such encroachments?

(a) whether Government is aware that unseasonal and early rainfall during the peak mango season in recent years has adversely impacted mango productivity and quality, if so, the assessment made in this regard;
(b) whether Government, in coordination with the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) and State Agricultural Universities, has undertaken research and development initiatives to evolve climate-resilient and off-season mango varieties; and
(c) whether any dedicated research centres, field trials or pilot projects have been established to develop and promote off-season mango cultivation practices, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether the Ministry has taken any action on the representation submitted for studying the legislations of Belize and Ireland aimed at ensuring timely delivery of justice and whether relevant legal insights or best practices from these jurisdictions have been examined for adoption;
(b) whether Government has received any representation or proposal specifically focused on guaranteeing timely justice in the country; and
(c) if so, the details thereof and the concrete steps taken or proposed to address this critical issue?

(a) whether Government proposes to enact a comprehensive legislation to provide statutory framework for the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR) to enhance accountability, governance and global competitiveness in an innovation-driven economy;
(b) whether Government has conducted any independent evaluation of CSIR’s research output, commercial impact and return on public funds, including how research priorities of individual CSIR laboratories are determined;
(c) whether such legislation would mandate performance and outcome audits, disclose revenues generated through technology transfer; and
(d) whether international best practices, including Korea’s Science and Technology Act, are being studied to bridge India’s competitiveness gap with leading R&D nations?

(a) whether Government maintains or proposes to strengthen the collection of State-wise statistical data on persons with intellectual disabilities in the country and the steps taken in this regard;
(b) whether Government has assessed the number of homeless persons with intellectual disabilities, including those abandoned by their families and the welfare, rehabilitation, healthcare and social protection measures being implemented for their dignified care; and
(c) whether Government, in collaboration with State Governments, proposes to formulate an integrated and time-bound action plan to further strengthen rehabilitation and social inclusion for such persons and the broad outline thereof?

(a) whether Ministry has taken cognizance of serious safety concerns in road tunnels, including accidents in the Mumbai Coastal Road tunnel and tunnel collapses in Telangana and Uttarakhand, endangering workers;
(b) whether Ministry has reviewed the adequacy of existing safety standards and maintenance protocols for tunnels;
(c) whether international best practices, including tunnel safety management regulations followed in Hong Kong, have been examined in this regard; and
(d) whether Ministry, in coordination with National Disaster Management Authority, proposes to frame uniform national regulations for tunnel safety applicable to Railways, National Highways and State Highways to prevent recurrence of such incidents?

(a) whether Government has taken note of persistent inter-State variations in enforcement of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, despite advisories issued by FSSAI;
(b) whether Government has undertaken a nationwide assessment of deficiencies in food-testing laboratories, including shortages of trained manpower, lack of modern testing facilities and low conviction rates in food adulteration cases across States;
(c) whether institutional reforms, including a nationally coordinated food safety implementation policy and strengthened enforcement mechanisms, are under consideration; and
(d) whether legislative or financial measures are proposed to ensure uniform implementation of food safety laws across States, bridge infrastructure and human resource gaps?

(a) whether Government has examined and acted upon the suggestions/representations received for infusing greater efficiency, reliability, and accountability in the country’s statistical data systems;
(b) whether international best practices and relevant foreign laws in this field have been studied and, if so, the key measures identified for improving the accuracy and credibility of statistical data;
(c) the details of steps taken to ensure collection of reliable statistical data and systematic correction and updation of inaccurate data; and
(d) the details of measures initiated to strengthen accountability mechanisms in Government data collection and dissemination processes?

(a) the reason for not enhancing the fund amount of the MPLAD funds though it has been found that the 5 crores annual allocation is a small amount for development;
(b) whether Government is inclined to increase the quantum of this amount in the coming years;
(c) whether Government is planning to enhance the amount for the Rajya Sabha Members considering the vast area that a Rajya Sabha member represents; and
(d) the difficulties, hurdles to consider this matter positively as it has been brought to the notice of Government in the past also?
J

(a) whether the Ministry has taken specific steps to ensure maximum work productivity from each Railway employee, including structured motivational initiatives and advanced technical training;
(b) whether any transparent, technology driven system has been introduced to regularly assess, rationalise the workload of each Railway employee;
(c) the action taken to fill vacancies of doctors and paramedical staff in Railway hospitals and dispensaries; and
(d) whether the Ministry has formulated or implemented any comprehensive plan to deploy Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Railway functions such as safety, maintenance, human resource management, medical services and passenger interface, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government is aware that children residing with women prisoners in jails face serious challenges relating to nutrition, healthcare, emotional well-being and access to childhood education and if so, the steps taken by Ministry, in coordination with the Ministry of Home Affairs and State Governments, to address these concerns;
(b) whether any nationwide study or assessment has been conducted to evaluate the living conditions, welfare standards and developmental needs of such children; and
(c) whether updated jail-wise data on number of children living with women prisoners is available and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?

(a) whether Government is aware of the significant untapped potential of fish farming in upstream dam and reservoir areas for livelihood generation and additional revenue to the Union and State Governments and if so, the present status thereof;
(b) whether any specific policy, guidelines or scheme has been formulated to promote scientific fish farming in such areas and if so, the details thereof; and
(c) if not, whether Government proposes to frame a dedicated policy, in consultation with State Governments and other stakeholders, to harness this potential and if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government proposes to direct fertilizer manufacturing units to replace obsolete vessels, machinery and process equipment to strengthen public safety, given that such plants handle hazardous chemicals and are situated near densely populated areas;
(b) if so, the details thereof, including prescribed timelines for compliance and applicable statutory safety standards;
(c) whether the fixed cost policy has been revised to incorporate updated fixed cost norms covering mandatory safety up-gradation;
(d) the present status of fixed cost matters pending since 2014, the steps taken for their resolution; and
(e) whether periodic third-party safety audits and incentives for technology up gradation are under consideration?
bpn

(a) whether Government is aware that modern, cost-effective, disaster-resilient and sustainable housing construction technologies developed by various Central agencies are not being adequately adopted or mainstreamed in villages and Tier-II and Tier-III cities;
(b) whether the Central Government, in coordination with State Governments proposes to promote large-scale adoption of such modern construction technologies and innovative housing designs in rural and semi-urban areas; and
(c) specific measures taken or proposed, including policy interventions, capacity building of local bodies and artisans and technical landholding of States, to ensure that benefits of advanced housing technologies translate into affordable, safe housing at the grassroots level?

(a) whether Government is aware of the need for repair, strengthening and upgradation of the perimeter/compound wall at Nanded airport, the State of Maharashtra;
(b) whether, in coordination with Government of Maharashtra, the Central Government has taken steps to upgrade fire safety systems, aircraft refuelling facilities and strengthen the runway infrastructure at the said airport;
(c) the details of funds sanctioned and works completed or under progress in this regard; and
(d) whether Government proposes to increase flight operations and air connectivity from Nanded airport and if so, the details thereof?

(a) the steps taken by Railways to minimize energy consumption with a view to reduce the cost of operations, including adoption of energy-efficient technologies;
(b) whether any action has been taken or is under consideration to shift towards gas-fuelled or alternative fuel locomotives;
(c) whether the Railways is monitoring energy consumption and undertaking regular overhauling and energy audits of each electric and diesel locomotive; and
(d) the manner in which energy consumption is being minimized at railway stations, factories and maintenance depots and whether any centralized or real-time monitoring system exists for tracking energy usage at each such unit?

(a) whether Government is preparing a comprehensive and time-bound action plan for development of a Shalivahana-era cultural tourism corridor connecting historical, religious and cultural sites in the Marathwada region of Maharashtra, including Nanded, Ajanta-Ellora, Grishneshwar, Parli Vaijnath, Aundha Nagnath, Paithan, Lonar Lake and the Godavari-Manjira-Haridra confluence;
(b) whether this initiative includes components such as roads, railways, accommodation, digital guide, promotion of local handicrafts and employment generation; and
(c) whether Government is considering its national and international promotion and implementation of a special financial package by constituting a joint committee in coordination with Maharashtra, Karnataka and Telangana?

(a) whether the Ministry, in consultation with the Ministry of Health, the Department of Pharmaceuticals, State Governments and other relevant stakeholders, has taken any step to establish a comprehensive and coordinated regulatory mechanism for veterinary medicines, in view of the following parts (b) to (d);
(b) the involvement of multiple agencies in approval, manufacture and monitoring, leading to regulatory overlap and gaps;
(c) the absence of effective inter-ministerial and inter-agency coordination;
(d) concerns regarding quality control, availability, pricing, misuse and antimicrobial resistance; and
(e) whether Government proposes to issue unified guidelines or introduce legislative measures to strengthen regulation, improve coordination and safeguard animal health?

(a) whether it is aware that a substantial section of India’s population relies on spectacles and contact lenses for daily activities, thereby making accurate vision correction a critical public safety and public health concern;
(b) whether the absence of a uniform national regulatory mechanism for optometric services, covering eye testing, prescription accuracy, lens quality, fitting practices and professional accountability, may adversely affect vision health and contribute to road accidents; and
(c) whether Government proposes to examine international best practices and consider establishing a statutory National Optical Regulatory Board to ensure quality?

(a) whether an Ayush hospital is being sanctioned in Nanded district Maharashtra, if so, the hurdles for beginning the construction work of the proposed Ayush hospital;
(b) whether the district administration and the State Governments are expediting the process of completing the Ayush hospital at Nanded in case it is sanctioned and the relevant papers are complete as per the law;
(c) the timeline for completion of the aforesaid Ayush hospital in Nanded; and
(d) the district-wise list of Ayush Hospitals in Maharashtra and whether a scope exists for establishing 2-3 Ayush hospitals in Nanded district?
BPN

(a) whether Government is aware that frequent seismic changes and related geophysical activities adversely impact the structural strength and stability of buildings;
(b) if so, whether the Central Government, in consultation with State Governments and other relevant stakeholders, has assessed the risks of sudden building collapse arising therefrom;
(c) the details of existing guidelines or standards relating to structural safety and seismic resilience of buildings; and
(d) whether Government proposes to frame regulations mandating periodic structural and seismic safety audits of buildings to minimise the risk of sudden collapse and the details thereof?

(a) whether Government intends to introduce a Vande Bharat Express service from Amritsar to Nanded via New Delhi to facilitate Sikh pilgrims visiting Takht Shri Hazur Sahib and other religious destinations;
(b) whether Government also proposes to start a Vande Bharat Express between Nanded and Hyderabad to improve regional connectivity;
(c) what action plan has been formulated to augment the number and frequency of train services to and from Nanded, considering growing passenger demand; and
(d) whether Government intends to establish modern mechanised coach cleaning and maintenance facilities at Nanded, if so, the timeline thereof?

(a) whether the Ministry is aware that millions of Generation Z users increasingly rely on Artificial intelligence (AI)-based astrology platforms for emotional reassurance, personal decision-making and self-reflection, beyond mere predictions;
(b) whether Government has assessed the risks arising from such platforms, including misleading claims, opaque algorithms, excessive monetization, data-privacy concerns and possible financial exploitation of vulnerable users; and
(c) the specific steps taken, in coordination with concerned Central Ministries, regulatory authorities and State Governments, to regulate the functioning of AI-driven astrology platforms and to prevent citizens from being financially cheated?

(a) whether Government conducts any outcome-based assessment of cases handled by lawyers engaged by District, State and National Legal Services Authorities, including data on cases won, lost or withdrawn;
(b) whether the quality of pleadings, arguments and courtroom representation by such lawyers is independently evaluated through audits or random supervision;
(c) whether Legal Services Authority lawyers actively seek urgent hearings, interim reliefs or stay orders on behalf of beneficiaries, at par with private counsel;
(d) the steps taken to ensure effective/quality legal aid to needy persons; and
(e) whether any transparent mechanism exists to remove persistently underperforming legal aid lawyers?

(a) whether Government is aware that the livestock sector in the country, despite its vast potential for employment generation, farmers’ income enhancement, nutritional security and export growth, remains largely inadequately regulated;
(b) whether Government has examined international best practices, livestock development legislation of Republic of Korea and Vanuatu for industrial development of the livestock sector; and
(c) if so, whether Government proposes to formulate a comprehensive national policy or legislation for livestock development in consultation with State Governments, Veterinary Councils, veterinary institutions and other stakeholders to promote regulated growth, investment, if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government is aware that nearly 18 crore Indians suffer from anal-region disorders such as piles, fissure, fistula, rectal prolapse and related conditions;
(b) whether these disorders significantly impair quality of life, productivity and public health outcomes;
(c) whether India presently lacks India-specific scientific data, national registries, biobanks and dedicated research infrastructure for such highly prevalent conditions, despite advanced research being undertaken in developed countries; and
(d) whether Government proposes to establish a National Anal-Health and Anorectal Research Mission for research, prevention, early diagnosis and affordable treatment in the interest of public health and safety of human lives?

(a) the present status of examination of the recommendations of the Expert Group constituted under NITI Aayog for revision of energy norms for urea manufacturing units, proposed to be implemented from 1st April, 2025;
(b) whether the said Expert Group has submitted its report and the likely timeline for notification and implementation of the revised energy norms; and
(c) whether Government proposes to take expeditious and conclusive steps to resolve the long-pending issue relating to reimbursement of fixed costs to eligible Public Sector Undertaking and cooperative urea manufacturing units, with a view to strengthening their financial sustainability, improving operational efficiency and ensuring uninterrupted availability of fertilizers to farmers?

(a) whether Government is aware that the river Godavari, revered as the "Dakshin Ganga", has immense religious, cultural, agricultural significance, and whether Government intends to undertake rejuvenation of the entire stretch of the river Godavari, particularly at Nanded where Takht Sachkhand Shri Hazur Sahib is located, in view of pollution affecting water quality and ritual bathing;
(b) whether Government proposes to launch a dedicated national programme titled "NAMAMI GODAVARI" on the lines of Namami Gange for cleaning of the entire river Godavari; and
(c) if so, the details thereof, including coordination with the State Governments of Maharashtra and Telangana as on date?

(a) whether the Directorate General Factory Advice Service and Labour Institutes proposes to introduce mandatory norms for periodic replacement of aging vessels and equipment in chemical factories after completion of their certified safe life, in the interest of public safety;
(b) whether Government has reviewed the credibility of third-party safety audits for renewal of factory licences;
(c) whether any statutory civil or criminal liability is fixed on third-party safety auditors in cases of faulty certification leading to fatal industrial accidents; and
(d) whether Government proposes to frame a uniform national factory safety policy to ensure uniform safety standards?

(a) the project-wise updated status of all proposed and ongoing expansion projects of the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE), including its subordinate units, Boards and PSUs, along with approved costs and capacity targets;
(b) whether Government has reviewed the timelines for timely completion of these projects and adopted measures to prevent time and cost overruns;
